  "units": [
    {
      "id": "u1",
      "title": "Hangul I: Core Sounds",
      "description": "Learn the first 6 vowels and 10 consonants, then read real words.",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "u1-l1",
          "title": "Vowels I",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "Korean is written in Hangul, an alphabet made of simple shapes. Each letter is one sound.",
            "Letters are stacked into square blocks. A vertical vowel (ㅏ ㅓ ㅣ) sits to the right of the consonant, and a horizontal vowel (ㅗ ㅜ ㅡ) sits below it.",
            "When a block starts with ㅇ, that ㅇ is silent. So ㅇ + ㅏ = 아 is just the sound 'a'."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅏ",
              "target": "ㅏ",
              "say": "아",
              "roman": "a",
              "en": "'a' as in father",
              "note": "The short stroke points right. Open your mouth wide: 'ah'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅣ",
              "target": "ㅣ",
              "say": "이",
              "roman": "i",
              "en": "'ee' as in see",
              "note": "A single straight line, like the letter 'i'. Smile slightly: 'ee'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅜ",
              "target": "ㅜ",
              "say": "우",
              "roman": "u",
              "en": "'oo' as in moon",
              "note": "The stroke points down. Round your lips: 'oo'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅗ",
              "target": "ㅗ",
              "say": "오",
              "roman": "o",
              "en": "'o' as in go",
              "note": "The stroke points up. Round your lips: 'oh'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅓ",
              "target": "ㅓ",
              "say": "어",
              "roman": "eo",
              "en": "'uh' as in cup",
              "note": "The stroke points left. Open your mouth, relaxed: 'uh'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅡ",
              "target": "ㅡ",
              "say": "으",
              "roman": "eu",
              "en": "'oo' with flat lips",
              "note": "Say 'oo' but pull your lips flat, as if smiling."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u1-l2",
          "title": "Consonants I",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "Consonant shapes often show how your mouth or tongue moves. ㄴ looks like the tongue touching the top of the mouth.",
            "To practise, the app says each consonant together with the vowel ㅏ (for example 가), because consonants can't be spoken alone."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄱ",
              "target": "ㄱ",
              "say": "가",
              "roman": "g",
              "en": "'g' as in go (sometimes 'k')",
              "note": "Between g and k. At the start of a word it sounds soft, like 'g'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄴ",
              "target": "ㄴ",
              "say": "나",
              "roman": "n",
              "en": "'n' as in no",
              "note": "Tongue touches the ridge behind your top teeth."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅁ",
              "target": "ㅁ",
              "say": "마",
              "roman": "m",
              "en": "'m' as in mom",
              "note": "The shape is a closed mouth: lips together."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅅ",
              "target": "ㅅ",
              "say": "사",
              "roman": "s",
              "en": "'s' as in sun",
              "note": "Looks like a tent. Before 'ee' it sounds like 'sh'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅇ",
              "target": "ㅇ",
              "say": "앙",
              "roman": "ng",
              "en": "silent at the start, 'ng' at the end",
              "note": "At the start of a block it is silent. At the bottom of a block it is 'ng' as in sing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u1-l3",
          "title": "Consonants II",
          "kind": "letter",
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄷ",
              "target": "ㄷ",
              "say": "다",
              "roman": "d",
              "en": "'d' as in do (sometimes 't')",
              "note": "Between d and t. Tongue behind the top teeth."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄹ",
              "target": "ㄹ",
              "say": "라",
              "roman": "r",
              "en": "between 'r' and 'l'",
              "note": "A quick tongue tap, like the 'tt' in butter. At the end of a block it is closer to 'l'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅂ",
              "target": "ㅂ",
              "say": "바",
              "roman": "b",
              "en": "'b' as in boy (sometimes 'p')",
              "note": "Between b and p. Looks like a cup."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅈ",
              "target": "ㅈ",
              "say": "자",
              "roman": "j",
              "en": "'j' as in jam",
              "note": "Soft 'j', between j and ch."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅎ",
              "target": "ㅎ",
              "say": "하",
              "roman": "h",
              "en": "'h' as in hat",
              "note": "A light breathy 'h'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u1-l4",
          "title": "Build Words",
          "kind": "word",
          "intro": [
            "Now you can read real words. Sound out each block from left to right, top to bottom.",
            "Example: 바다 = 바 (ba) + 다 (da) = ba-da, which means sea."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-na",
              "target": "나",
              "roman": "na",
              "en": "I / me (informal)"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ai",
              "target": "아이",
              "roman": "a-i",
              "en": "child"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-namu",
              "target": "나무",
              "roman": "na-mu",
              "en": "tree"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-bada",
              "target": "바다",
              "roman": "ba-da",
              "en": "sea"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-saja",
              "target": "사자",
              "roman": "sa-ja",
              "en": "lion"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-moja",
              "target": "모자",
              "roman": "mo-ja",
              "en": "hat"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-gogi",
              "target": "고기",
              "roman": "go-gi",
              "en": "meat"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-dubu",
              "target": "두부",
              "roman": "du-bu",
              "en": "tofu"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-uri",
              "target": "우리",
              "roman": "u-ri",
              "en": "we / our"
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-dari",
              "target": "다리",
              "roman": "da-ri",
              "en": "leg / bridge"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "goals": [
        "read"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u1b",
      "title": "Your First Words",
      "description": "네, 아니요, 서울, 감사합니다: the four words you already know.",
      "goals": [
        "speak",
        "read"
      ],
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "u1-l5",
          "title": "Words You Already Know",
          "kind": "word",
          "intro": [
            "You already know four Korean words. Now you can read them.",
            "네 = ㄴ + ㅔ ('e' as in net). 아니요 = 아 + 니 + 요. 서울 = 서 + 울. 감사합니다 = 감 + 사 + 합 + 니 + 다.",
            "Notice that 합니다 is written 'hab-nida' but said 'ham-nida'. The ㅂ sound changes to 'm' before ㄴ. Korean spelling shows the letters, but speech often blends them."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ne",
              "target": "네",
              "roman": "ne",
              "en": "yes",
              "note": "Said like 'neh'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-aniyo",
              "target": "아니요",
              "roman": "a-ni-yo",
              "en": "no",
              "note": "Polite 'no'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-seoul",
              "target": "서울",
              "roman": "seo-ul",
              "en": "Seoul",
              "note": "The capital of Korea. ㅓ is 'eo'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-gamsa",
              "target": "감사합니다",
              "roman": "gam-sa-ham-ni-da",
              "en": "thank you",
              "romanAlt": [
                "gamsahabnida"
              ],
              "note": "Formal and polite. Works everywhere."
            }
          ],
          "introSpeak": [
            "You already know four Korean words. Let's practise saying them clearly.",
            "Say 감사합니다 as 'gam-sa-ham-ni-da'. The 'ham' sounds like 'hahm'. The ㅂ before ㄴ turns into an 'm' sound.",
            "네 is 'neh' (yes). 아니요 is 'ah-nee-yo' (no). 서울 is 'suh-ool' (Seoul)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "title": "Hangul II: Complete the Alphabet",
      "description": "Y-vowels, double vowels, puffed and tense consonants, and final consonants.",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "u2-l1",
          "title": "Y-Vowels",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "Adding a second short stroke to a vowel adds a 'y' glide in front. ㅏ becomes ㅑ (ya)."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅑ",
              "target": "ㅑ",
              "say": "야",
              "roman": "ya",
              "en": "'ya' as in yacht",
              "note": "ㅏ with an extra stroke."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅕ",
              "target": "ㅕ",
              "say": "여",
              "roman": "yeo",
              "en": "'yuh' as in young",
              "note": "ㅓ with an extra stroke."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅛ",
              "target": "ㅛ",
              "say": "요",
              "roman": "yo",
              "en": "'yo' as in yo-yo",
              "note": "ㅗ with an extra stroke. You hear it at the end of polite sentences."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅠ",
              "target": "ㅠ",
              "say": "유",
              "roman": "yu",
              "en": "'you'",
              "note": "ㅜ with an extra stroke."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u2-l2",
          "title": "Vowels II",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "ㅐ and ㅔ sound almost identical in modern Korean. Don't worry about telling them apart by ear. You will learn them by spelling."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅐ",
              "target": "ㅐ",
              "say": "애",
              "roman": "ae",
              "soundGroup": "e",
              "en": "'e' as in bed",
              "note": "Sounds the same as ㅔ for most speakers today."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅔ",
              "target": "ㅔ",
              "say": "에",
              "roman": "e",
              "soundGroup": "e",
              "en": "'e' as in bed",
              "note": "Sounds the same as ㅐ for most speakers today."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅘ",
              "target": "ㅘ",
              "say": "와",
              "roman": "wa",
              "en": "'wa' as in water",
              "note": "ㅗ + ㅏ glide together."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅝ",
              "target": "ㅝ",
              "say": "워",
              "roman": "wo",
              "en": "'wuh' as in wonder",
              "note": "ㅜ + ㅓ glide together."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u2-l3",
          "title": "Puffed Consonants",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "Four consonants are 'aspirated': pronounced with a strong puff of air. Hold your hand in front of your mouth and you should feel it."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅋ",
              "target": "ㅋ",
              "say": "카",
              "roman": "k",
              "en": "'k' with a puff of air",
              "note": "ㄱ with an extra stroke."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅌ",
              "target": "ㅌ",
              "say": "타",
              "roman": "t",
              "en": "'t' with a puff of air",
              "note": "ㄷ with an extra stroke."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅍ",
              "target": "ㅍ",
              "say": "파",
              "roman": "p",
              "en": "'p' with a puff of air",
              "note": "Looks like a table, and you pop a big 'p'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅊ",
              "target": "ㅊ",
              "say": "차",
              "roman": "ch",
              "en": "'ch' as in church",
              "note": "ㅈ with an extra stroke."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u2-l4",
          "title": "Tense Consonants",
          "kind": "letter",
          "intro": [
            "Five doubled consonants are 'tense': squeeze your throat and say them with no puff of air, like a sharp stop."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄲ",
              "target": "ㄲ",
              "say": "까",
              "roman": "kk",
              "en": "tight 'g/k', no puff",
              "note": "Like the 'k' in sky."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㄸ",
              "target": "ㄸ",
              "say": "따",
              "roman": "tt",
              "en": "tight 'd/t', no puff",
              "note": "Like the 't' in sty."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅃ",
              "target": "ㅃ",
              "say": "빠",
              "roman": "pp",
              "en": "tight 'b/p', no puff",
              "note": "Like the 'p' in spy."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅆ",
              "target": "ㅆ",
              "say": "싸",
              "roman": "ss",
              "en": "tight 's'",
              "note": "A sharper, hissing 's'."
            },
            {
              "id": "ko-ㅉ",
              "target": "ㅉ",
              "say": "짜",
              "roman": "jj",
              "en": "tight 'j'",
              "note": "A tense 'j', like 'ch' with no puff."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u2-l5",
          "title": "Final Consonants (Batchim)",
          "kind": "word",
          "intro": [
            "A consonant at the bottom of a block is called batchim (받침). It closes the syllable.",
            "Only 7 sounds are possible at the bottom: k (ㄱ ㅋ ㄲ), n (ㄴ), t (ㄷ ㅅ ㅈ ㅊ ㅌ ㅎ), l (ㄹ), m (ㅁ), p (ㅂ ㅍ), ng (ㅇ).",
            "Example: 밥 = ㅂ + ㅏ + ㅂ = bap. The final ㅂ is a quick closed 'p'."
          ],
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "ko-bap",
